course: dictionary
lesson: 7001
title: Many meanings of parallel
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Parallel lines are straight lines that always stay the same distance apart and never meet, like train rails. In maths, this is the main meaning, and you will see it with rectangles and marked arrows.
explain: Parallel can also mean happening at the same time or in the same way. The clue is usually in the words around it. Read «If a rip carries you out, stay calm and swim parallel to the shore.» Parallel to the shore means sideways, along the beach. Try the swap check: if you swim straight out or straight in, the sentence no longer makes sense for safety.
explain: A parallel is a likeness between two things, so the word can be a noun too. Words like «in parallel» and «parallel to» show you how to use it. A parallel circuit gives electric current more than one path, so the word can also mean a way things are connected. When the meaning is unclear, check the words around it, then use a dictionary.
fact: Parallel lines are straight lines that always stay the same distance apart and never meet.
fact: «Parallel to» means in the same direction as, and never touching, a line or shore.
fact: In a parallel circuit, if one bulb breaks, the others stay on.
fact: In geography, lines of latitude are called parallels, such as «the 49th parallel» between the USA and Canada.
fact: A parallel is a likeness between two things, and parallel can also mean happening at the same time.
exercise: «Rectangles have two pairs of parallel sides.» How many pairs of parallel sides does a rectangle have? | Three | Four | One | *Two
exercise: «If a rip carries you out, swim parallel to the shore.» What does parallel to the shore mean? | *Sideways, along the beach | Straight back in to the sand | Under the water | Straight out to sea
exercise: In geography, what is a parallel? | *A line of latitude on a map | Two lines that cross at a right angle | A type of electric bulb | A rip current near the shore
test: Which description fits parallel lines? | A zigzag path up a hill | A curve that bends back on itself | Lines that cross at a right angle | *Straight lines that never meet
test: «In a parallel circuit, if one bulb breaks, the others stay on.» What happens to the other bulbs? | *The others stay on | The circuit explodes | All the bulbs go out
exam: Ben says, «Our two stories have a parallel: both are about a brave dog.» What does parallel mean here? | *A likeness between two things | A rip current near the shore | A path for electric current | Two straight lines that never meet
